B|Which structure remains covalently continuous immediately after a monofunctional glycosylase releases uracil? |A DNA strand with an AP site|A strand with a one-nucleotide gap|Two separate strands with double-strand breaks|A DNA strand with a new cytosine|A|Only the N-glycosidic bond is hydrolyzed. A backbone break requires another reaction; cytosine replacement requires synthesis.
B|A typical CPD joins which residues?|Opposite purines|Adjacent pyrimidines on the same strand|Two phosphates|Pyrimidines on different strands|B|The cyclobutane ring connects adjacent pyrimidines on one strand. Interstrand crosslinks and phosphodiester bonds are different structures.
B|Which cofactor directly participates in the photochemical electron-transfer cycle of CPD photolyase?|NAD⁺|ATP|FADH⁻|Coenzyme A|C|Reduced flavin transfers an electron after light excitation and is regenerated. ATP is not the direct energy source for splitting the dimer.
B|What is the consequence of an MGMT transfer event for the repair protein?|It receives a nucleotide|It is regenerated by light|It hydrolyzes ATP to recover|Its active cysteine is irreversibly alkylated|D|The acceptor protein is stoichiometrically inactivated. This differs from the catalytic cycles of photolyase and AlkB.
B|Which lesion is a standard substrate of MGMT?|O6-methylguanine|Uracil opposite G|8-oxoG opposite C|A CPD|A|MGMT removes the O6 alkyl group. Uracil and oxidized guanine typically enter BER; CPDs can undergo photoreversal or NER.
B|The correct order for a simple monofunctional BER reaction is which?|Ligase → glycosylase → polymerase → APE1|Glycosylase → AP incision → end processing/synthesis → ligation|UvrA → UvrB → UvrC → ligase|Polymerase → ligase → glycosylase → APE1|B|Base removal produces the substrate for incision, which generates a repairable end. The Uvr sequence belongs to NER.
B|Which chemical group is needed for polymerase extension?|3′ phosphate|5′ hydroxyl|3′ hydroxyl|A free base alone|C|DNA polymerase extends a 3′ hydroxyl. A 3′ phosphate blocks extension, and a 5′ phosphate is relevant to final ligation.
B|Which protein is a principal mammalian AP endonuclease?|XPC|UvrC|MGMT|APE1|D|APE1 incises an AP site; XPC is a NER recognition factor, UvrC is bacterial NER nuclease, and MGMT directly transfers alkyl groups.
B|Which repair protein removes A opposite 8-oxoG in the bacterial GO system?|MutY|MutT|MutM|UvrD|A|MutY removes inappropriate adenine. MutM targets oxidized guanine and MutT acts on oxidized nucleotide triphosphates.
B|The function of MutT is best described as what?|DNA ligation|Nucleotide-pool sanitization|NER incision|Photoreactivation|B|MutT hydrolyzes an oxidized nucleotide triphosphate before incorporation. It is not a chromosomal glycosylase.
B|Which enzyme can remove a 5′-dRP residue during short-patch mammalian BER?|Pol ε only|XPG|Pol β|Photolyase|C|Pol β supplies dRP lyase as well as polymerase activity. XPG incises NER substrates and photolyase performs direct reversal.
B|What is the principal function of FEN1 in long-patch BER?|Base flipping|Direct alkyl transfer|ATP-dependent photochemistry|Cleavage of a displaced DNA flap|D|Displacement synthesis creates a flap whose removal permits formation of a ligatable nick. FEN1 does not select the initial damaged base.
B|What does a bifunctional DNA glycosylase additionally possess?|AP lyase activity|RNA polymerase activity|Ligase activity|Photolyase activity|A|Bifunctional glycosylases can cleave at the AP intermediate through elimination chemistry, sometimes leaving blocked ends.
B|Which E. coli NER protein supplies the two incision activities?|UvrA|UvrC|UvrD|DNA Pol I|B|UvrC is the nuclease. UvrA assists surveillance, UvrD facilitates fragment removal and Pol I performs repair synthesis.
B|The usual bacterial NER excision oligomer is approximately what length in E. coli?|1 nt|3 nt|12–13 nt|100–200 nt|C|The characteristic fragment is about 12–13 residues. Mammalian NER usually removes a longer 24–32-nt oligomer.
B|Which eukaryotic protein is associated with global-genome NER initiation?|CSA only|MutH|Pol β|XPC|D|XPC participates in global recognition; CSA helps transcription-coupled recognition. MutH and Pol β belong to different mechanisms.
B|Which nuclease cuts on the 5′ side of a lesion in human NER?|XPF–ERCC1|XPG|APE1 in every NER event|FEN1|A|XPF–ERCC1 makes the 5′ incision; XPG makes the 3′ incision. APE1 and FEN1 are not the canonical NER incision pair.
B|Which event initiates transcription-coupled NER in a mammalian protein-coding gene?|Binding of a ribosome|Stalling of RNA polymerase II at a template lesion|Semiconservative replication initiation|Methylation of an MGMT cysteine|B|The stalled transcription complex recruits coupling factors. Replication initiation and translation are distinct processes.
B|Which repair mechanism requires no DNA repair-patch synthesis for its defining reaction?|Long-patch BER|Global NER|Photolyase-mediated CPD reversal|UvrABC-mediated NER|C|Photolyase restores the linked bases directly. Both BER and NER rebuild nucleotide material.
B|Which description of human DNA photolyase is accurate?|It is the principal human NER enzyme|It is a human mitochondrial ligase|It repairs every UV lesion after visible light|Humans lack functional DNA photolyase repair|D|Placental mammals lack this direct pathway. Human cryptochromes do not establish active DNA photoreactivation.
AR|Assertion: BER can transiently increase strand-break signal. Reason: Incision at an AP site introduces a backbone discontinuity.|Both true; reason explains assertion|Both true; reason does not explain assertion|Assertion true; reason false|Assertion false; reason true|A|AP incision is a normal repair intermediate, so break signal may rise before synthesis and ligation.
AR|Assertion: An MGMT molecule can repeatedly repair O6-methylguanine without replacement. Reason: The active-site cysteine is irreversibly alkylated.|Both true; reason explains assertion|Both true; reason does not explain assertion|Assertion true; reason false|Assertion false; reason true|D|Irreversible alkylation explains why the assertion of repeated turnover is false.
AR|Assertion: A CPD can be removed by NER in darkness. Reason: NER removes a lesion-bearing oligonucleotide without needing photochemical cleavage of the CPD.|Both true; reason explains assertion|Both true; reason does not explain assertion|Assertion true; reason false|Assertion false; reason true|A|Excision does not require photolyase excitation. DNA patch synthesis replaces the damaged region.
AR|Assertion: MutT hydrolyzes an oxidized nucleotide triphosphate. Reason: Human XPG cuts on the 3′ side of an NER lesion.|Both true; reason explains assertion|Both true; reason does not explain assertion|Assertion true; reason false|Assertion false; reason true|B|Both statements are accurate but describe unrelated biochemical steps.
C|A U:G duplex undergoes complete BER initiated by uracil glycosylase. Which pair is restored?|T:A|A:T|C:G|U:A|C|The intact G directs incorporation of C. Repair follows the complement, not the identity of the excised U.
C|A U:A duplex instead undergoes complete BER. Which newly incorporated base is expected opposite A?|G|C|U|T|D|A templates T during DNA repair synthesis. The same glycosylase can therefore lead to different correct products.
C|A lesion is removed efficiently but AP sites persist at high levels. A defined monofunctional BER system is used. Which step is most likely defective?|AP incision|Photolyase excitation|UvrD displacement|Initial glycosylase recognition|A|Base loss indicates initiation is functional, while accumulation of the immediate product implicates its consumption. Other causes require controls in cells.
C|A mutant Pol β retains normal synthesis on a clean gap but fails on a 5′-dRP substrate. Which activity is most directly implicated?|NER lesion verification|dRP lyase|Photoreactivation|3′-side NER incision|B|The difference between substrates is the residual dRP group, not the capacity to add nucleotides.
C|An extract removes the base lesion, incorporates the expected replacement nucleotide, but retains a nick with compatible ends. Which missing activity best explains this?|DNA glycosylase|UvrA|Ligase|MGMT|C|The initial lesion and missing nucleotide are resolved; ligation is the necessary remaining chemical step.
C|A damaged duplex is restored in light by purified enzyme without dNTPs, and no oligonucleotide is released. Which mechanism is most consistent?|Long-patch BER|Nucleotide excision|Mismatch repair|Direct photoreversal|D|The dependence on light and lack of patch synthesis point to photolyase. Excision routes need synthesis for full restoration.
C|A cell has 350 active MGMT molecules and 500 suitable lesions, with no replenishment or competing reactions. What is the ideal minimum unresolved lesion count?|150|350|500|0|A|Each protein corrects at most one lesion. 500 − 350 = 150; actual unresolved count may be greater.
C|If the same cell makes 100 additional active MGMT molecules during recovery, what is the upper bound on the number of corrected lesions?|100|450|500|350|B|Total active molecules available are 350 + 100 = 450; the initial 500 lesions do not limit that smaller total.
C|An E. coli uvrC-deficient strain binds bulky lesions normally but generates no characteristic excision oligomers. Which interpretation is best?|UvrC is the initial glycosylase|UvrC supplies the ATP for photolyase|Dual incision is impaired|The initial DNA is undamaged|C|UvrC catalyzes the cuts after recognition and verification; recognition alone does not establish excision.
C|A purified Uvr system forms both incision products, yet the excised fragment remains associated with DNA. Which missing protein is most plausible?|MutT|MGMT|UvrA only|UvrD|D|UvrD facilitates displacement and repair-complex turnover. Its absence does not imply that UvrC cannot incise.
C|A mammalian cell shows near-normal global-genome lesion removal but defective recovery of RNA synthesis. Which defect fits best?|CSB-dependent coupling|Both core NER nucleases completely absent|All ligases absent|All glycosylases absent|A|Selective transcription-associated impairment can spare much global NER. A complete core nuclease defect would affect both recognition branches.
C|A cell lacks XPC but retains functional transcription coupling and core NER. What is a reasonable prediction?|Both recognition branches are necessarily absent|Global recognition is impaired, but some transcription-coupled repair remains|Direct reversal is impossible for all lesions|All BER stops|B|The recognition branches differ before convergence; XPC is principally involved in global-genome initiation.
C|A labeled lesion-bearing strand produces a 28-nt released product after incubation with a mammalian extract. What is the strongest interpretation if the normal control shows no such band?|One nucleotide has been directly reversed|The product is an entire chromosome|Damage-directed NER-like excision has occurred|Ligation must be complete|C|The product length and damage dependence support excision. They do not independently establish gap filling and closure.
C|The labeled nucleotide lies 50 bases from a lesion in a substrate undergoing 25-nt NER excision. Why may the released oligomer be invisible to that label?|NER never removes DNA|The label must be a protein|The lesion is necessarily absent|The label lies outside the excised tract|D|A distant label can remain in a parental fragment. Label placement determines which products are visible.
C|After recovery, lesion signals are 20 and 60 from initially equal 100 on template and non-template strands. Which fractions were removed?|80% and 40%|20% and 60%|40% and 80%|80% and 60%|A|Subtract remaining signals from 100. Preferential template removal supports transcription-associated repair, not unequal damage induction.
C|A first-order lesion burden falls from 600 to 150 in 30 min. What is the apparent half-time?|30 min|15 min|10 min|60 min|B|150/600 = 1/4, representing two half-times in 30 minutes. This assumes constant apparent first-order removal.
C|Two independent first-order removal routes have k = 0.02 and 0.03 min⁻¹. What is the combined apparent half-time?|34.7 min|23.1 min|13.9 min|50 min|C|Add the constants to obtain 0.05 min⁻¹, then calculate ln(2)/0.05 = 13.86 min. The assumption is independent competition.
C|Fifty short-patch events and ten NER events with 25-nt patches complete. How many nucleotides are incorporated?|60|250|75|300|D|50 × 1 + 10 × 25 = 300. Event count and incorporated nucleotide count are different.
C|A 450-nm photon has approximately what energy?|4.41 × 10⁻¹⁹ J|4.41 × 10⁻²⁸ J|266 J per photon|450 J per photon|A|E = hc/λ; convert 450 nm to 4.50 × 10⁻⁷ m. About 266 kJ/mol is the corresponding molar photon energy, not single-photon energy.
C|A cell with MutY deficiency accumulates A opposite 8-oxoG. Which mutation is favored if the A becomes a template?|C:G to G:C only|G:C to T:A|A:T to G:C necessarily|No possible mutation|B|A templates T during a later copy, creating the transversion from the original G:C. Actual spectra reflect additional pathways too.
C|A population loses 80 of 100 initiating lesions but has 40 AP sites and 30 later intermediates. Assuming all sites are accounted for, how many repairs are complete?|80|30|10|20|C|20 initial lesions remain; 20 + 40 + 30 = 90 unresolved sites. Only ten are fully repaired.
C|Which statement set is correct? I. MGMT consumes active protein. II. Photolyase requires repair synthesis. III. APE1 incises AP sites.|I only|II and III only|I and II only|I and III only|D|I and III are correct. Photolyase restores the covalent structure directly without a replacement patch.
C|Match proteins to functions: P. FEN1; Q. XPG; R. MutT; S. UvrD. 1. 3′ NER incision; 2. flap cleavage; 3. oxidized dNTP hydrolysis; 4. fragment displacement.|P2 Q1 R3 S4|P1 Q2 R4 S3|P3 Q4 R1 S2|P4 Q3 R2 S1|A|Each function corresponds to a distinct chemical or structural substrate; XPG is not the flap nuclease and MutT does not displace DNA.
C|Two UV-sensitive cell lines have normal initial lesion burdens. Line A shows low unscheduled DNA synthesis; line B has normal excision but poor replication past remaining CPDs. Which pair of mechanisms fits?|Both direct reversal only|A: NER defect; B: lesion tolerance defect|A: translation defect; B: BER exclusively|Both necessarily XPC defects|B|Repair synthesis reflects excision-dependent reconstruction; polymerase eta-dependent tolerance can fail despite functional excision.
C|A double mutant loses both photolyase and UvrC. In a CPD assay, why can the double mutant retain more damage than either single mutant?|UvrC creates CPDs|Photolyase makes AP sites|Two overlapping removal routes are impaired|The two enzymes form one obligatory active site|C|Both can act on CPDs through different chemistry. Removing both alternative opportunities can increase persistence.
C|An AP lyase leaves a 3′ phosphate end. What should happen before gap synthesis?|Add another phosphate to the 3′ end|Seal the blocked end immediately|Remove the entire complementary strand|Convert the 3′ end to a hydroxyl|D|The blocked phosphate must be processed before polymerase can extend. PNKP is one relevant mammalian activity.
C|A researcher claims a cell has repaired every UV lesion because CPD-specific signal is zero. Which additional concern is decisive?|Other UV photoproducts and unresolved repair intermediates may remain|CPDs are not made by UV|Every UV lesion is a CPD|CPD removal always creates a mutation|A|A lesion-specific assay cannot detect all other photoproduct classes or completion of repair.
C|A bulky lesion lies on the non-template strand of an actively transcribed gene. Which prediction is most defensible?|It must stall RNA polymerase directly in every case|It may still be repaired by global NER even without template-strand stalling|It cannot be repaired|It must be converted to uracil|B|Global recognition surveys both strands; transcription coupling specifically begins with an obstruction on the strand being read.
